🔀 Path-Based Routing Logic

The Application Load Balancer listens for incoming client requests on port 80 and switches traffic dynamically based on the requested URL route [image_Hh8SjC.png]:

URL Path Pattern	Destination Target Group	Target Service Node	Host Availability Zone
/train/*	tg-train-service	Train Service EC2	ap-south-1a
/plane/*	tg-plane-service	Plane Service EC2	ap-south-1b
/bus/*	tg-bus-service	Bus Service EC2	ap-south-1c


Request Ingress Flow

1. A client initiates an HTTP request (e.g., http://<ALB-DNS>/plane/index.html) over the internet [image_Hh8SjC.png].
2. The Application Load Balancer intercepts the request and parses the path rules [image_Hh8SjC.png].
3. Finding a match for /plane/*, the ALB routes the request to the tg-plane-service Target Group [image_Hh8SjC.png].
4. The Target Group hands the traffic over to the Plane Service EC2 instance running inside the ap-south-1b Availability Zone [image_Hh8SjC.png].

🧩 Architecture Components


1. Application Load Balancer (ALB)

• Acts as the single public entry point for all mobile and desktop clients [image_Hh8SjC.png].
• Terminates external client connections and handles routing rules natively at Layer 7.

2. Target Groups

Three independent Target Groups are provisioned to isolate service traffic [image_Hh8SjC.png]:
• tg-train-service: Binds the Train application instance.
• tg-plane-service: Binds the Plane application instance.
• tg-bus-service: Binds the Bus application instance.

3. Compute Layer (Amazon EC2)

Independent EC2 servers host the distinct transport application stacks [image_Hh8SjC.png]:
• Train Service Node: Running inside subnet ap-south-1a.
• Plane Service Node: Running inside subnet ap-south-1b.
• Bus Service Node: Running inside subnet ap-south-1c.

⚙️ Implementation Steps

1. Network Initialization: Provision an AWS Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) configured with three public subnets mapped to ap-south-1a, ap-south-1b, and ap-south-1c respectively [image_Hh8SjC.png].
2. Gateway Mapping: Connect an Internet Gateway to the VPC and configure route tables to allow internet connectivity [image_Hh8SjC.png].
3. Firewall Configurations:
	• Create an ALB Security Group allowing inbound HTTP traffic on port 80 from 0.0.0.0/0.
	• Create an EC2 Security Group allowing inbound port 80 traffic only from the ALB Security Group.
4. Compute Provisioning: Launch 3 distinct EC2 instances across the subnets, deploying application runtimes (e.g., Nginx/Apache hosting mock pages) on each node [image_Hh8SjC.png].
5. Target Group Allocation: Define tg-train-service, tg-plane-service, and tg-bus-service Target Groups and register their respective EC2 nodes [image_Hh8SjC.png].
6. ALB Deployment: Spin up an internet-facing Application Load Balancer across all three subnets [image_Hh8SjC.png].
7. Rule Configuration: Bind an HTTP listener on port 80 to the ALB and write path-pattern rules matching /train/*, /plane/*, and /bus/* to point traffic to the correct Target Group [image_Hh8SjC.png].

🧪 Testing and Validation

Validate infrastructure endpoints by executing the following URL paths against the ALB public DNS name:
• http://<YOUR-ALB-DNS-NAME>/train/ (Should return the Train Service page)
• http://<YOUR-ALB-DNS-NAME>/plane/ (Should return the Plane Service page)
• http://<YOUR-ALB-DNS-NAME>/bus/ (Should return the Bus Service page)

Verification Metrics:

• Confirm that active requests to one URL path route correctly without causing overhead on the other two nodes.
• Verify via the AWS Console that all three Target Groups report their registered EC2 targets as Healthy via standard HTTP check paths.

🔐 Security Considerations

• Restricted Access Control: Backend compute instances remain isolated from the direct public internet; they accept incoming HTTP calls exclusively from the ALB proxy.
• Minimal Blast Radius: Compromising or breaking one application service node does not provide direct internal access to the compute environments of the other services.

📈 Key Architectural Benefits

• High Availability & Fault Isolation: Distributing workloads natively across ap-south-1a, 1b, and 1c guards against full data center outages [image_Hh8SjC.png]. If an entire AWS zone suffers a hardware or utility failure, the remaining two services continue running uninterrupted.
• Cost Efficiency: A single load balancer multiplexes incoming traffic across multiple applications, optimizing billing overhead by removing the need for dedicated routing hardware for every single microservice.
• Decoupled Lifecycle: The Train, Plane, and Bus environments maintain structural independence [image_Hh8SjC.png]. System administrators can run patches or deploy updates on the Train node without taking down the Bus or Plane services.

